Z||000000||Richard Parnell||05-21-2000||10:30 PM||||The winners are ta da...Patrick and Phaeton-  I woke up in the middle of the night and it struck me right between the eyes. You see I'm not too bright while awake.. Someone had said something about the diameter of the slave and master must be the same.  Sure enough when I measured the diameter of my new slave cylinder it was 15/16 as compared to the master which is 13/16.  I put the old 13/16 slave back on and it's pretty funky with the ATF expanded seal but it works- barely.  I have to grind the thing into reverse when driving and it's hard to shift but I can drive it if I want to. What is odd is I have bought 4-5 slave cylinders and have found at least 4 different types from parts houses and none that match the original.  But every single master I have bought is the same exact cylinder.<BR>I think that the diagnosis of the pressure plate being on it's last legs still holds.  But thanks guys at least I can take my baby to work (only 2 stops lights on the way).  Going into town would not be an option but to work-ok.  The V8 project will still move ahead.  I'm not doing anything to the present clutch and pressure plate.  I'm so happy I can't believe I'm so attached to that %&$#ing car. Now I will have a beer.||||reg||6
Z||000001||Patrick||05-22-2000||04:46 AM||||<BR>Richard, this is as good a place as any to apologize for putting pressure on you to try the ATF in the first place many months ago. Although I did suggest to you to try ATF with the <I>old</I> slave and master cylinders, I still feel bad that you and many others had so much trouble after adding ATF to your clutch hydraulic systems. I'm grateful that I found out about trying ATF because it did the trick for me, but it's now obvious that ATF isn't something to use with <I>new</I> clutch hydraulic components.<P>I hope that I've helped to even the score somewhat with my comments on slave cylinder diameters. Good luck.<P> <P> ||||reg||1
